this episode is brought to you by the  through the web podcast a podcast that i  co-host and talk about all things  science tech and business check it out  in the description below  hi  welcome to another episode of cold  fusion  using our legs to do an activity is  something that those of us who are fully  mobile take for granted every day  those who have suffered a full spinal  cord injury have unfortunately had their  lives changed forever being a paraplegic  presents a set of lifelong challenges  that the rest of us never really ponder  but what if there was a way to reverse  this  well a team of scientists have made the  first tentative steps towards a feasible  solution in this episode we'll take a  look  you are watching  tv  this technology is so precise  that immediately after the surgery the  patient can walk and stand  in a clinical trial three men were  recently given the opportunity to walk  again thanks to a new technology  showcased in the journal of nature  medicine the system is a spinal cord  implant that stimulates muscles allowing  them to move again  gregoire cortine a neuroscientist at the  swiss federal institute of technology  and co-leader of the study stated that  this technology was for the most serious  form of spinal cord injury  quote  that means individuals with clinically  complete spinal injury no sensation no  movement in the legs  he also added that the technology could  be used for anyone with a spinal cord  injury that occurred above the lower  ribcage  so  how exactly does this technology work  when the spinal cord is broken the  signals from the brain can't reach the  target muscles so limbs don't move and  no sensation is felt  currently this is usually irreversible  but the thing is the motor neurons below  the injury are still there they're just  inactive  to reactivate the muscle neurons again  the research group used modified  implants originally used for pain  management  in essence it was the repurposing of an  older technology used to block chronic  pain by supplying electrical current to  neurons but this time instead of  blocking the pain the electrical current  would be used to stimulate muscle  neurons and make the muscles move  the paper states that previous efforts  would target the dorsal column of the  spinal cord but this approach decided to  go to the spinal dorsal roots  the researchers believe that this would  provide better restoration of more  diverse movement in the most severe  cases of spinal injury  so giving the neurons in the muscles an  electrical current is one thing but it  has to be done just right  in fact the system has to simulate the  electrical messages from the brain so  the timing of the signals has to be done  precisely and done in such a way that it  makes the patient's leg muscles contract  in a coordinated manner that resembles a  human step  in the paper they refer to this as a  quote natural activation pattern  as the research paper puts it quote  targeting the individual dorsal roots  with a predefined timing can reproduce  the natural activation pattern of motor  neurons during walking end quote  but how are they going to figure out how  to interface the system with the right  neurons within the body  well it's actually pretty interesting  how they went about it  first the team of scientists took mri  images of 27 spines and then fed that  into an algorithm  the algorithm then produced a predictive  model of the average spinal cord this  included the size and a map of the  layout of neurons  so what was this magic algorithm well  it's a program that already exists and  has for a little while it's called sims  for life  the program could generate finite  element models of the spine generate the  nerve layouts as well as simulate the  voltages and electrical current in the  neurons  amazingly these predictions meant that  they could test the feasibility of their  systems without trying it out on a live  patient it could all be optimized and  evaluated in simulation and i think  that's pretty cool  after some more tweaking and simulation  they came up with the final design  next it was time to install the device  neurosurgeons implanted three men with  the device all of the participants had  their spinal cords completely cut in  separate motorbike accidents and were  completely paralyzed  after 10 days of recovery after surgery  the men started training with the device  and accompanying software  amazingly the simulations beforehand in  the software proved to be accurate  all three participants recovered some  level of movement within just one day of  activating the implant this included  being able to walk on a treadmill while  their weight was supported  one of the patients mikel roccati states  quote the first few steps were  incredible a dream come true  it wasn't only walking though the  participants were also able to do  activities such as peddling a bicycle  and performing squats the device guided  their muscles through all the movements  it should be noted that the patients  aren't fully walking yet  but as reggie edgerton an exercise  physiologist at the university of  california who was also working on  spinal cord injury solutions told nature  quote the next question is whether  stimulation over a long time will help  the motor neurons grow the correct  connections to carry out the movements  without stimulation  end quote  we'll have to wait and see this could be  big if it does come to pass  right now the system has 16 electrodes  but a higher number of electrodes say 32  could provide higher resolution movement  the team plans to move testing into the  united states and has already received  approval from the fda they're going to  test the system in a us-based clinical  trial with more people  the researchers plan to focus on people  with more recent injuries because tests  done in rodents have shown that animals  tend to recover more motion when the  stimulation begins immediately after  their injury  they're also testing a brain implant  which would directly route mental  instructions to the spinal cord  stimulation machinery this device might  restore other functions lost due to  spinal cord injury such as bladder  control  so there's a lot of work being done in  the space as reggie edgerton says the  work will give many people with spinal  cord injuries reason to have a lot of  hope  but there should be some pause  first of all it's still an invasive  spinal surgery which comes with its own  risks though other scientists are  working on another method which uses  electrodes applied to the skin to  activate the spinal neurons externally  it would be cheaper and less risky  as for mikhail roccati he's very happy  with the results so far quote i'm using  it now every day for a couple of hours  at work in my home to do different kinds  of things now it's a part of my daily  life it's good  working is super important but just  stand up  can solve a lot of kind of problems in  the normal life  maybe just to do a simple shower  without crutches i can stand up and do a  shower  with the walker  i'm free i can walk wherever i want  at this moment  okay now i'm working  [Music]  there are so many patients around the  world  who need a treatment to fight against  paralysis  we have reached  the point where we are ready to develop  this treatment this way we are  collaborating with onward medical that  is developing all the technology to turn  this result  into a treatment available throughout  the world  there you have it  we may have just witnessed the first  steps to a mobile life to those with  severe spinal injuries it's great to see  so what do you guys think about this  i'm personally hoping for much more  progress over the coming decade  if you'd like to discuss this in more  detail head on over to the coldfusion  discord  also don't forget to check out my  podcast through the web in the  description  so thanks so much for watching  my name is dagogo and you've been  watching cold fusion and i'll catch you  again soon for the next episode  cheers guys  have a good one  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  oh
 

